Note that the detailed ChangeLog is always available using the
[Development/Recent activity](/src/?history) menu on the web site, as
well as in the [/src/ChangeLog]() file in the distribution.

# Release '20-10-26 (gview)'

## Usage of LLMs for coding

This release is probably the first where a substantial number of
patches were written with the assistance of LLMs (Large Language
Models), either by me or by others. This is also the occasion to
remind Basilisk's contributors of a few basic principles:

* while LLMs are clearly powerful assistants for programming, they are
  also very ressource hungry, so one needs to use them "responsibly"
  i.e. "with moderation" (like alcohol, lifts, cars and other addictive
  substances).
* the human author of the code is still entirely responsible for the
  content produced, including bugs, potential violations of copyright,
  non-recognition of the contributions of others etc. This is
  unfortunately even more true given the irresponsible and unethical
  attitudes of the companies producing these tools.
* "Responsible use" means that the author must check and understand
  every line of code produced by the LLM. This is essential for the
  long-term maintainabilty of Basilisk.
* And of course, I (or others) should not have to guess that an LLM
  has been used to produce a submitted patch. This should be declared
  up front.

Aside from these principles, I can share a few comments from personal
experience using LLMs (mostly ChatGPT) to code the new gview
interface (and some of the GPU drivers):

* LLMs are clearly useful as "experts" to give general directions in
  the "jungle" of available libraries etc. For example the overall
  architecture of gview was designed with both my input (i.e. previous
  experience with jview etc.) and that of the LLM for the precise
  choice of which libraries to use.
* LLMs can give accurate summaries of how some of the (undocumented)
  parts of Basilisk work (for example the GPU drivers), but they can
  also be misleading and/or inaccurate, and it is difficult to judge
  the level of confidence of the output from the text produced.  
* The "efficiency" or "productivity" gained from using LLMs for
  programming can be difficult to evaluate. For example, I would say
  that the initial version of gview was clearly produced much faster
  (~1 day) than without a LLM, however the final version took approx
  ~5 days of very largely manual debugging and refining.
* A somewhat perverse side-effect is that LLMs can distract you (by
  making it look easy) from the "really useful things" you should be
  doing (and which cannot be done by an LLM): for example one could
  say that using a week to write gview was not the best use of my time
  (and I would not have done it if the LLM had not been able to code
  the initial draft quickly).
* Note also that none of the present document was written using an
  LLM: I consider writing (text not code) as a task which should not
  be delegated to an LLM.

## gview

[Gview](/src/gview/display.c) is a new interactive interface which
replaces the [jview interface](/src/jview/README). There are two
versions, compiled from the same C99 source code: a desktop linux
application (`gview-gui`) and a
[WebAssembly](https://webassembly.org/) version running directly in
the browser, on any system. The initial version was written with the
help of ChatGPT 5.6.

## GPUs

* The multilayer solver now runs well on GPUs thanks to the [remapping
  implementation in C](/src/layered/remapc.h) of Antoine Aubert, this
  replaces the PPR Fortran library.
* The [GPU grids](/src/grid/gpu/grid.h#see-also) now support a wide
  range of libraries/hardware: CUDA for nvidia cards, AMD HIP for AMD
  cards, OpenCL for a broad range of accelerators/GPUs, Metal for
  MacOSX etc. With special thanks to Hugo Jacquet and Edoardo Cipriano
  (and LLMs).
* 3D multigrid is now possible on GPUs thanks to Andreas Iberl (and
  LLMs). See for example the [3D isotropic turbulence
  example](/src/examples/isotropic.c#running-on-gpus).
* A better memory (re)allocation scheme: 512^3^ [3D isotropic
  turbulence](/src/examples/isotropic.c) is possible with a 24GB
  RTX4090 GPU (16 GB are used).

## Weighted Load Balancing

Riccardo Caraccio has implemented a generalised weighted Load
Balancing which can for example be used to automatically partition
based on CPU load rather than number of grid points. See [this
example](/src/test/balance-rotate.c).

## Other

* a new example: the [Held & Suarez climate model](/src/examples/held-suarez.c).
* another new example: [turbulent flow in a channel with a "wall
  model"](/src/examples/wall-model.c).  
* New [msolve()](/src/solve.h#multiple-unknowns) macro solves generic
  coupled linear systems, see the [Ginzburg-Landau
  example](/src/examples/ginzburg-landau.c#using-the-generic-linear-system-solver).
* The wiki does not allow files larger than 1MB.
* [Calculation of the face fraction](/src/fractions.h#face-fraction)
  from the PLIC interface, thanks to Jose Lopez-Herrera, Gabriele
  Gennari and Edoardo Cipriano.

## Bug fixes

* Fixed sign of the implicit source of embed_flux for degenerate cells
  thanks to Robert A and Edoardo Cipriano.
* Bug fix for axi + embed thanks to David Pekker.
* Many other more minor fixes.

# Release '25-02-26 (macros)'

## Macros in qcc and rewrite of grid implementations

The preprocessor and the `grid/*` implementations have been rewritten
using the new [macro](ast/macro.h) functionality. As a consequence the
older macro definitions, typically using the syntax

~~~literatec
@def foreach_mymacro()
...
@
@define end_foreach_mymacro()
~~~

will not work anymore and need to be rewritten using the
simpler/cleaner/better new syntax.

In the same context, the special `map()` macros, used in the previous
implementation for coordinate mapping are now obsolete. See
[spherical.h]() for an example on how to use the new [macro
inheritance](ast/macro.h#inheritance) feature to implement this
mapping.

Similarly expressions using

~~~c
Point point = ...;
~~~

which have caused 'obsolete' warnings for a while, will now trigger an
error. They should be replaced with `foreach_point/foreach_region`
which are compatible with reductions, GPUs etc.

The `solve()` macro [has been rewritten](solve.h) using the new macro
functionality and is now a [return macro](ast/macro.h#return-macro)
i.e. its syntax has changed.

## Generalised support for GPUs

Most of the rewrite above was motivated by a better integration of
GPUs. Several important test cases and examples now run on GPUs
including:

* [Stommel gyre](test/stommel-ml.c)
* [Isopycnal gyres intersecting the bathymetry](test/bleck.c)
* [The Gulf Stream](examples/gulf-stream.c)

New benchmarks results were done on a high-end "gamers" graphics card
[RTX4090](grid/gpu/Benchmarks.md): performances for realistic
applications (e.g. the Gulf Stream) of a single RTX4090 card are
comparable to that of the 768 AMD cores of the cluster at d'Alembert.

## Non-square/cubic domains on multigrids

It was previously possible to define non-square/cubic domains only when
using `grid/multigrid` and MPI. This now works also in OpenMP/serial
and on GPUs. See the [updated Tips](Tips#non-cubic-domains).

Note also that all "dimensions/aspect ratios" must now be specified
(previously some dimensions were automatically guessed by the MPI
topology layout engine): this change was made because the previous
behaviour could cause confusion (i.e. the aspect ratio could change
when one was not careful to use the appropriate number of MPI
processes).

## Flexible homogeneous boundary conditions

The definition of "homogeneous" versions of boundary conditions is now
flexible (previously it required modification of the AST qcc
engine). See [Basilisk C documentation](/Basilisk%20C#homogeneous-boundary-conditions).

## Other changes

* [lines()](draw.h#lines-from-a-file.) draws lines from a file
* [vectors()](draw.h##vectors-displays-vector-fields) take a "level"
  optional argument
* [dump()](output.h#dump-basilisk-snapshots) has a new 'zero' option
  to avoid dumping empty fields
* [Wasm support](README.wasm)
* [Global tides examples](examples/global-tides.c)
